@page "/admin/knowledge-base"
@layout CMSLayout

<PageTitle>@L["Nav_KnowledgeBase"]</PageTitle>

<div class="page-header">
    <h1>@L["Nav_KnowledgeBase"]</h1>
    <p>@L["KB_Subtitle"]</p>
</div>

<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px;border:1px solid var(--cms-danger);background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--cms-danger) 8%, transparent)">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0;color:var(--cms-danger)"><i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill"></i> @L["KB_XssWarningTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text)">@L["KB_XssWarningBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Sitemap language coverage ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Found while testing a default-language switch: a page that exists only in a
   non-default language never gets its own sitemap section. Documented here
   rather than "fixed" — tying section identity to whichever language a page
   happens to be authored in first would make section URLs shift every time a
   translation is added, which is a worse trade-off than the gap itself. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-diagram-3"></i> @L["KB_SitemapLanguageTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_SitemapLanguageBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Language: pages vs. templates ───────────────────────────────────────────
   The two look like the same field (a Language picker) on two different admin
   screens, but they do genuinely different jobs — a page's language drives
   real infrastructure (sitemap hreflang, the Language Switcher block, URL
   prefixing); a template's is filtering/bookkeeping only. Written up after a
   user asked, mid-session, whether the two were the same mechanism. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-translate"></i> @L["KB_LanguageTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LanguagePagesSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_LanguagePagesBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LanguageTemplatesSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_LanguageTemplatesBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Templates: linked vs copied ─────────────────────────────────────────────
   Written after a linked template got edited on the homepage and the edit went
   nowhere: the public site and the editor both replace a linked wrapper's
   content with the template's current content, so the page's own copy of it is
   never read. The editor now locks it; this explains why, and what the two
   toolbar buttons on a locked template do. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="templates">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-layout-text-window"></i> @L["KB_TemplatesTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_TemplatesIntro"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_TemplatesLinkedSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_TemplatesLinkedBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_TemplatesCopySubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_TemplatesCopyBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_TemplatesWhichSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_TemplatesWhichBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Site codes on one page ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Every site code runs everywhere; the page editor's "Site Kodları" dialog is
   where one page opts out. Documented because the natural first guess is the
   other way round — "add this code to this page" — and that is the custom-code
   block, not this. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="site-codes-per-page">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-code-square"></i> @L["KB_SiteCodesPerPageTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_SiteCodesPerPageBody"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_SiteCodesPerPageWarning"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Form attachments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Written with the feature: a public upload is the one place a site takes bytes
   from strangers, so what is accepted, where it goes and who can open it should
   be readable by the person who puts a file field on a form. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="form-attachments">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-paperclip"></i> @L["KB_FormAttachmentsTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_FormAttachmentsBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_FormAttachmentsLimitsSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_FormAttachmentsLimitsBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_FormAttachmentsWhereSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_FormAttachmentsWhereBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Lighthouse ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   What a Lighthouse run on the live site found, and which of it is the site's
   content (the operator's to fix) versus the platform (already handled) versus
   the server (nginx). Written after a run that scored 85/86/100/92 and traced
   every red item to one of those three. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="lighthouse">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-speedometer2"></i> @L["KB_LighthouseTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_LighthouseIntro"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LighthouseImagesSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_LighthouseImagesBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LighthouseContrastSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_LighthouseContrastBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LighthouseButtonsSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_LighthouseButtonsBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LighthouseThirdPartySubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_LighthouseThirdPartyBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_LighthouseServerSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_LighthouseServerBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Responsive images ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The media library links here from its own header. Written up rather than
   turned into more UI because the interesting part is what the site does on its
   own after an upload — which is invisible from the library screen. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="responsive-images">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-images"></i> @L["KB_ResponsiveImagesTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesWhatBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesHowSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesHowBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesAltSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesAltBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesPasteSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesPasteBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesCompressionSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_ResponsiveImagesCompressionBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Two-letter code ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Written up after a user asked whether two English variants (GB/US) could be
   configured — they can, today, with no code change, because this field was
   always allowed to hold more than two characters. Documents why the field is
   named the way it is despite that, and what actually reads it. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="two-letter-code">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-globe2"></i> @L["KB_TwoLetterCodeTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_TwoLetterCodeNameBody"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_TwoLetterCodeLengthBody"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_TwoLetterCodeRegionBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Skip link ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Why it targets the first <h1> instead of jumping straight to #main-content:
   a linked header template resolves INTO the page's own HTML at render time
   (see TemplateResolutionService), so the header ends up as <main>'s literal
   first child — landing on #main-content itself would put a keyboard user
   right back on the header they were trying to skip. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-arrow-bar-right"></i> @L["KB_SkipLinkTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_SkipLinkBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Redirects import ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The Redirects screen links here from its own import dialog. Written up rather
   than crammed into that dialog because the column format and the "empty new
   path = 410" rule are worth having at hand independent of that one screen visit. *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="redirects-import">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-signpost-split"></i> @L["KB_RedirectsImportTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_RedirectsImportFormatBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_RedirectsImportGoneSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_RedirectsImportGoneBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_RedirectsImportValidationSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_RedirectsImportValidationBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Integration secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The "Sırlar" tab on Site Settings links here from its own intro banner. Written
   up rather than said in full there because the "why a separate table" reasoning
   is worth having at hand independent of that one screen visit — see
   IntegrationSecret, IntegrationSecretsBootstrap, IntegrationSecretsConfigurationProvider
   and IBlobStorageFactory for the code side (everything here applies live, no
   restart — see IIntegrationSecretsReloader/IIntegrationSecretsChangeNotifier for
   how a save propagates, including across to the Web app for CAPTCHA). *@
<div class="cms-card" style="margin-bottom:16px" id="secrets">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock"></i> @L["KB_SecretsTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_SecretsWhatBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_SecretsWhySeparateSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_SecretsWhySeparateBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_SecretsWhoSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_SecretsWhoBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_SecretsFileSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_SecretsFileBody"]</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px">@L["KB_SecretsRestartSubtitle"]</p>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_SecretsRestartBody"]</p>
</div>

@* ── Restore procedure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Deliberately documentation rather than a button: restoring overwrites the live
   database, and a one-click path to that is worth less than the risk it carries.
   The steps live here so they are at hand when they are actually needed. *@
<div class="cms-card" id="restore-guide">
    <h4 style="margin-top:0">@L["KB_RestoreTitle"]</h4>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted)">@L["KB_RestoreBody"]</p>
    @* The comments inside this block are the only part that changes per language
       (see CmsMessages*.resx, KB_RestoreCommands) — the commands themselves are
       copy-pasted into a terminal verbatim and must stay identical in every
       language, so they are NOT part of the translated string. *@
    <pre style="padding:12px;background:var(--cms-bg);border-radius:6px;font-size:12px;overflow-x:auto"><code>@L["KB_RestoreCommands1"]
docker compose stop cms web

@L["KB_RestoreCommands2"]
docker compose exec db dropdb -U elevare ElevareDB
docker compose exec db createdb -U elevare -O elevare ElevareDB
docker compose exec cms pg_restore -h db -U elevare -d ElevareDB --no-owner \
    "/app/Backups/&lt;@L["KB_RestoreCommandsFileHint"]&gt;"

@L["KB_RestoreCommands3"]
docker compose start cms web</code></pre>
    <p style="color:var(--cms-text-muted);font-size:13px;margin-bottom:0">@L["KB_RestoreAfterNote"]</p>
</div>
